## Why This Is Asked
Tests understanding of per-env isolation, simple gating, and multi-provider setups for real-world tools like Databricks and Snowflake in a beginner-friendly way.

## Key Concepts
- Env tagging across modules
- Per-env remote state backends
- Provider aliases for multi-environment isolation
- Lightweight CI gates for plan validation

## Code Example
```javascript
// backend snippet (per-env)
terraform {
  backend "s3" {
    bucket = "tf-state-bucket"
    key    = "env/dev/terraform.tfstate"
    region = "us-east-1"
    dynamodb_table = "tf-locks-dev"
  }
}

// wrapper module outline
module "env_wrapper" {
  source = "./modules/env-wrapper"
  environment = var.environment
  tags = { Environment = var.environment }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you extend gates for Snowflake/DATABRICKS resources?
- How would you test the gating logic locally before CI?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to simplify tagging, maintain per-env isolation, and introduce a safe migration path.

## Key Concepts
- Terraform provider default_tags
- Per-env backend prefixes
- Gate checks on plan output for tag presence
- Migration risk and rollback considerations

## Code Example
```terraform
provider "aws" {
  region = "us-east-1"
  default_tags = {
    tags = {
      Environment = var.environment
      CostCenter  = var.cost_center
    }
  }
}
```

```hcl
# migrated resource sample (no wrapper)
resource "aws_vpc" "main" { cidr_block = "10.0.0.0/16" }
```

```hcl
# backend example
terraform {
  backend "s3" {
    bucket = "corp-terraform-state"
    key    = "envs/${var.environment}/terraform.tfstate"
    region = "us-east-1"
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you handle prod tag overrides if needed?
- What tooling would you add to enforce tag presence across environments?
